<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oecusse</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Oecusse-Ambeno, Oe-Cusse Ambeno</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oecusse is an exclave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-timor">East Timor</a> on the north coast of the western, Indonesian half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timor">Timor</a>. Around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lifau">Lifau</a> it was the earliest centre of Portuguese presence on the island, and the border treaties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> of 1859 and 1914 left it as a Portuguese enclave within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Timor. It is now a special administrative region of East Timor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oecusse.jpg" alt="Oecusse" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Clementina de Oliveira · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mountains_in_Oecusse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Oecusse, also called Oecusse-Ambeno, is a municipality and the only special administrative region of Timor-Leste. It lies on the north coast of the western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timor">Timor</a> and is separated from the rest of the country by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-timor">West Timor</a>, which surrounds it on all sides except the north, where it faces the Savu Sea. Its area is 813.6 km2 and its capital is Pante Macassar. Ambeno was the historic Timorese kingdom that occupied most of the present territory, while Oecusse was the traditional name of Pante Macassar and its environs; the name Oecusse comes from the Uab Meto words for water and a clay jug, or perhaps a ruler named Kussi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Oecusse was among the first parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timor">Timor</a> where the Portuguese settled and is usually considered the place where Timor-Leste began. Around 1556 the Dominican friar António Taveiro began missionary work on the north coast, and in 1569 the village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lifau">Lifau</a> appears on a European map. In 1641 Dominican priests baptised the royal families of Ambeno, Mena and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanuban">Amanuban</a>. From the 1650s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topasses">Topasses</a>, a Eurasian population, grew in strength, controlled the sandalwood trade and used Lifau as a stronghold. In 1702 Lifau became the capital of the colony, with its first governor from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>; the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dili">Dili</a> in 1769 because of attacks by the Topass leader Francisco Hornay III.</p><p>The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> of 1859 divided the island between Portugal and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and left Oecusse as an enclave inside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> territory. In 1912 João da Cruz, the Liurai of Ambeno, revolted against the Portuguese; the revolt was put down and the Ambeno kingdom lapsed. Indonesian forces invaded Oecusse on 6 June 1975, a few months before the invasion of the rest of Timor-Leste. The region became part of the independent state on 20 May 2002.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Articles 5 and 71 of the 2002 constitution provide for a special administrative and economic regime, and a law of 2014 created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/special-administrative-regions-of-china">Special Administrative Region</a>. It has four administrative posts and 18 sucos, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">Census</a> of 15 October 2022 gave a population of 80,176. The Atoni Pah Meto are the dominant ethnic group and Uab Meto is the native language, while Tetum is the main language of public life; 99.3 per cent of inhabitants are Roman Catholic, many also following older customs known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adat">adat</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oecusse” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Oecusse</b> became a Special Administrative Region in 2015 and Atauro a municipality in 2022.</blockquote><a href="/empire/east-timor">East Timor · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Sandalwood gave way to coffee in the 19th century, and borders with the Dutch were settled in 1859 and 1914, leaving the <b>Oecusse</b> enclave.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-timor">Portuguese Timor</a></li></ul></section>
